{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791541729,"nonce":"nsGASFKWu8XPDX38+vz7Aon5DTE=","message":"Full-loop versioning has not landed. Two frozen LLVM-off NUC comparisons reject the cloning approach so far. Seven randomized repetitions per arm, affinity 0-7:\n\nFirst copy: operator 100K wall 45.258 baseline / 44.878 same-binary disabled / 47.474 enabled ms; 10M 92.565 / 93.347 / 97.960 ms. Main-frame reservation 624 -\u003e 1024 bytes; static whole-function floating spill stores 17 -\u003e 53 and loads 24 -\u003e 53. These counts include cold code and are not dynamic spill counts.\n\nSplitting short state latches and adding post-version memory cleanup: 100K 45.455 / 45.299 / 48.836 ms; 10M 93.568 / 93.518 / 100.821 ms. Frame 992 bytes. All 17 Lua and 22 supported Python outputs pass, as do state transfer/side-exit/alias/OSR regressions. No broad Python benefit established. CPU0 comparisons retain LuaJIT as substantially faster. ARM screening was invalidated by a concurrent Rayzor build and is discarded.\n\nThe versioning draft is set aside; it is not enabled in the working tree. Prototype sources are under /tmp/zyntax-virtual-prototype locally. Full NUC records remain under /tmp/zyntax-virtual-first-* and /tmp/zyntax-virtual-tail-*, with frozen binaries/manifests virtual-loops-first and virtual-loops-tail. A further conditional-error latch prototype is also uncommitted. Sharing/isolating cold paths and proving correlated virtual-state fields remain work; simply increasing the clone budget regresses both short and long wall time.\n\nInvestigation found a separate existing compiler capability missing from Lua: the pending-error global is not registered, and imported snapshot globals do not acquire the configured error_flag metadata. Fixing that integration is the next concrete step before further cloning.","files":null}]}